- check null
- final Object[] es = elements;
- es[head = dec(head, es.length)] = e;
- if (head == tail) grow(1);

So it puts e at new head position (decrement head). The "corresponding end" maybe the element at index tail (the end). Since tail is the position where next element would be added at the tail, the element at tail-1 (the last element) is the previous last element. After addFirst, the element at the tail (i.e., last element) should not be the same as e, unless deque was empty before? If deque was empty, after addFirst, head == tail? Actually initially head == tail (empty). dec(head) moves head back one slot, then set element there. Since head != tail after insertion (since we inserted one element), tail still points to empty slot after the element. The last element is at index tail-1 (circular). That element is e (since only one element). So tail element equals e. That would contradict "differs". So maybe they intend that the "corresponding end" (i.e., the element at the other end) should be different from the supplied element, unless the deque was empty before? Not clear.

Alternatively, maybe "corresponding end" refers to the element at the opposite end of the deque after operation, i.e., after addFirst, the element at the tail (last) should be different from the supplied element, unless the deque was empty before. The spec may need to capture that.

